<article class="env-situation" id="0BZB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BZB">Situation <span data-tag="0BZB">47.13.6</span>.</a> Let $R \to A$ be a ring map. We will give an alternative construction of $R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (A, -)$ which will stand us in good stead later in this chapter. Namely, suppose we have a differential graded algebra $(E, d)$ over $R$ and a quasi-isomorphism $E \to A$ where we view $A$ as a differential graded algebra over $R$ with zero differential. Then we have commutative diagrams </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \vcenter { \xymatrix{ D(E, \text{d}) \ar[rd] &  &  D(A) \ar[ll] \ar[ld] \\  &  D(R) } } \quad \text{and}\quad \vcenter { \xymatrix{ D(E, \text{d}) \ar[rr]_{- \otimes _ E^\mathbf {L} A} &  &  D(A) \\  &  D(R) \ar[lu]^{- \otimes _ R^\mathbf {L} E} \ar[ru]_{- \otimes _ R^\mathbf {L} A} } }  \]
</div>
  <p> where the horizontal arrows are equivalences of categories (Differential Graded Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/09S6" data-tag="09S6">22.37.1</a>). It is clear that the first diagram commutes. The second diagram commutes because the first one does and our functors are their left adjoints (Differential Graded Algebra, Example <a href="/tag/0BYX" data-tag="0BYX">22.33.6</a>) or because we have $E \otimes ^\mathbf {L}_ E A = E \otimes _ E A$ and we can use Differential Graded Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0BZ3" data-tag="0BZ3">22.34.1</a>. </p>
